# ------------------------------------------------------------------ History and politics
ps("V02-D-001", "History and politics",
   "The competence objection to choosing officials by lot wrongly compares lot with ideal expertise rather than with elections as they actually filter candidates.",
   "Medium",
   "Critics of choosing public officials by lot usually object that it ignores competence: a random citizen, they say, is no more fit to govern than a random passenger is to fly an aircraft. The objection assumes that election reliably selects for the relevant competence, and that assumption deserves more scrutiny than it receives. Elections reward the ability to win elections, which overlaps with the ability to deliberate well on public questions but is not the same thing; they also filter heavily for wealth, connections and an appetite for publicity. Lot has its own weaknesses, and nobody sensible proposes drawing a finance minister from a hat. But for bodies whose task is to weigh evidence and represent a cross-section of opinion, such as panels reviewing a contested policy, the comparison that matters is not lot against an ideal of expertise but lot against the electoral filter as it actually operates.",
   "The objection that lot ignores competence assumes elections select for it; for deliberative bodies, lot should be weighed against how elections really filter, not against ideal expertise.",
   [("Selection by lot is better than election for every public office, because elections favour wealth, connections and a taste for publicity over genuine ability.",
     "The paragraph limits its case to deliberative bodies and says 'nobody sensible proposes drawing a finance minister from a hat', so 'every public office' contradicts its stated scope."),
    ("Elections reward the skill of winning votes instead of the skill of deliberating, which is why elected officials are usually less competent than citizens chosen at random.",
     "The paragraph says the two abilities overlap 'but' are 'not the same thing'; it never claims elected officials are less competent than random citizens, so this overstates the point."),
    ("Since a random citizen is as unfit to govern as a random passenger is to fly, lot should be confined to advisory panels that hold no real authority.",
     "The passenger analogy is the critics' view, which the paragraph scrutinises rather than accepts, and it says nothing about denying such panels authority.")],
   "The paragraph reports the critics' objection, then attacks its hidden assumption that elections select for competence, noting that they reward 'the ability to win elections' and filter for wealth and publicity. It concedes lot's weaknesses and restricts its case to deliberative bodies, concluding that the right comparison is 'lot against the electoral filter as it actually operates'. The correct answer keeps the critique, the scope and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the option that turns a qualified critique of the objection into a sweeping endorsement of lot.",
   "V02D-sortition-and-competence",
   "Needs the author's scope limit (deliberative bodies only) to reject the sweeping option and to see that the passenger analogy belongs to the critics.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-D-002", "History and politics",
   "Wars are decided more by supply than by battlefield brilliance.",
   "Easy",
   "Popular accounts of war dwell on generals and battles, but campaigns are more often decided by supply. An army that cannot feed its soldiers, replace its ammunition or move fuel to its vehicles loses the capacity to fight long before it loses a battle. Many celebrated offensives stalled not because the enemy stopped them but because they outran the roads, railways or wagons that sustained them. Brilliant tactics can win an engagement; only a working system of supply can turn a run of engagements into a victory. This is why careful commanders tend to plan the movement of food and fuel before the movement of troops, and why the study of logistics, though unglamorous, explains more about the outcome of wars than the study of battlefield genius.",
   "Wars are usually decided less by tactical brilliance in battle than by keeping armies supplied, so logistics explains their outcomes better than generalship does.",
   [("Generals who win battles through clever tactics are overrated, since nearly every famous offensive failed once enemy forces managed to cut its lines of supply.",
     "The paragraph says offensives stalled 'not because the enemy stopped them' but because they outran their own supply, and it speaks of 'Many celebrated offensives', not nearly every one."),
    ("Careful commanders plan the movement of food and fuel before the movement of troops, and this habit makes them more successful than commanders who plan otherwise.",
     "This takes one illustrative detail as the point and adds a comparison of commanders that the paragraph never makes."),
    ("Popular histories of war ought to be rewritten to drop accounts of generals and battles and to concentrate instead on roads, railways and supplies.",
     "The paragraph makes a claim about what explains outcomes; it prescribes no rewriting of popular histories, and it grants that tactics can win engagements.")],
   "The opening sentence states the thesis that 'campaigns are more often decided by supply', the middle sentences support it, and the last sentence concludes that logistics 'explains more about the outcome of wars than the study of battlefield genius'. The correct answer restates exactly this comparison.",
   "Picking the option about commanders' planning because it repeats a detail from the final sentence.",
   "V02D-military-logistics",
   "The thesis is stated in the first and last sentences; wrong options are a misread detail, a narrow detail and an added prescription.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-003", "History and politics",
   "Premodern empires valued courier relays because faster information let rulers oversee distant officials.",
   "Medium",
   "Large premodern empires often invested heavily in relay systems of couriers and stations long before they built much else in common. The reason is less obvious than it seems. A ruler far from his provinces faced a problem not of force but of information: governors might rebel, under-report taxes or simply act on stale orders, and the centre could not correct what it could not see. A fast relay shortened the delay between an event and the capital's knowledge of it, and so shrank the room in which local officials could act unobserved. The roads and horses carried letters, but what they really delivered was the credible prospect of timely oversight. That is why such systems were often reserved for official use rather than opened to merchants and private correspondents.",
   "Premodern empires prized courier relays mainly because quicker news let distant rulers watch provincial officials, narrowing the room for disobedience or deceit to go unnoticed.",
   [("Premodern empires built courier relays chiefly to move troops and tax revenue quickly between capital and provinces, which made any rebellion much harder to sustain.",
     "The paragraph says the ruler's problem was 'not of force but of information' and that the relays 'carried letters', so troops and revenue misstate their purpose."),
    ("Because distant governors routinely rebelled and concealed taxes, premodern rulers came to treat fast relays as their only effective means of keeping control.",
     "The paragraph says governors 'might' rebel or under-report, not that they routinely did, and it never calls relays the only means of control."),
    ("Courier relays in premodern empires were closed to merchants because private letters would have crowded the roads and delayed the government's own messages.",
     "The paragraph explains the restriction by the relays' role in oversight; congestion of the roads is a reason it never gives.")],
   "The paragraph poses a puzzle (why relays came early), answers it by recasting the ruler's problem as one of information, and explains that speed 'shrank the room in which local officials could act unobserved'. Its last line, 'what they really delivered was the credible prospect of timely oversight', is the point the correct answer captures.",
   "Choosing the troops-and-revenue option because relays sound like military infrastructure.",
   "V02D-imperial-courier-relays",
   "The correct answer depends on the reframing from force to information; one wrong option uses the force reading and another inflates 'might' into 'routinely'.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-004", "History and politics",
   "The cartel and trust views of craft guilds can coexist; which function dominated likely depended on how hard quality was to observe.",
   "Difficult",
   "Two accounts of medieval craft guilds have long competed. On one, guilds were cartels: they restricted entry, fixed prices and kept outsiders from the trade, enriching masters at the expense of customers and would-be rivals. On the other, they were institutions of trust: in markets where buyers could not easily judge the quality of cloth or metalwork, a guild's standards and inspections gave its mark a meaning that no single workshop could establish alone. The two accounts are usually presented as rivals, but they describe mechanisms that can coexist in the same institution, and the more useful question is which one predominated under which conditions. Where quality was easy to observe, the trust function had little to add and restriction was likely to dominate; where quality stayed hidden until long after purchase, the same barriers to entry may have been the price of a reputation worth having.",
   "The cartel and trust views of guilds need not be rivals; which function prevailed probably depended on how hard buyers found it to judge quality before buying.",
   [("Guilds were primarily institutions of trust rather than cartels, since their limits on entry were simply the price buyers paid for reputations that benefited every customer.",
     "The paragraph refuses to choose one account and says restriction 'was likely to dominate' where quality was easy to observe, so siding wholly with trust contradicts it."),
    ("The cartel and trust accounts of guilds are both partly right, because every guild restricted entry to some extent and every guild also inspected its members' goods.",
     "The paragraph's point is conditional, that the balance depended on how observable quality was; it makes no universal claim about what every guild did."),
    ("Where the quality of goods could be judged at a glance, guilds served no useful purpose at all, and towns would have done better without them.",
     "The paragraph says only that the trust function 'had little to add' there; 'no useful purpose at all' and the verdict on towns go beyond it.")],
   "The paragraph sets out the cartel view and the trust view, then argues that they 'describe mechanisms that can coexist' and that the useful question is 'which one predominated under which conditions'. Its final sentence ties the answer to whether quality was easy to observe or 'stayed hidden until long after purchase'. The correct answer captures both the reconciliation and the condition.",
   "Choosing the 'both partly right' option, which sounds balanced but replaces the paragraph's conditional answer with an unsupported universal claim.",
   "V02D-medieval-craft-guilds",
   "Two options sound like reconciliations; only one keeps the condition (observability of quality) that decides between the mechanisms.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Economics and business
ps("V02-D-005", "Economics and business",
   "The advantage of entering a market first is conditional, and the anecdotes offered for it suffer from survivorship bias.",
   "Medium",
   "Business writing often treats being first to market as a decisive advantage, and the stories it tells are memorable: the pioneer that defined a category and never let go. Yet such stories are drawn disproportionately from survivors. Pioneers that created a market and were then overtaken by better-funded or more careful followers are forgotten, or are remembered as footnotes to the followers' success, so the sample from which the lesson is drawn is tilted in its favour. Followers also enjoy real benefits: they can learn from the pioneer's mistakes, wait for customers to understand the product and adopt technology that has become cheaper. This does not mean that entering first is never valuable; where customers face high costs of switching, an early lead can harden into dominance. It means that the advantage is conditional, and that the anecdotes usually offered for it cannot establish how often it holds.",
   "Entering a market first helps only in some conditions, such as high switching costs; belief in its power rests on stories that forget overtaken pioneers.",
   [("Firms that follow a pioneer into a new market generally outperform it, because they learn from its mistakes and adopt technology after it has become cheaper.",
     "The paragraph lists followers' benefits but never says they generally outperform pioneers; it says only that the first-mover advantage is conditional."),
    ("Entering a market first pays off only when customers find switching costly, and in every other market it is a handicap that sensible firms ought to avoid.",
     "Switching costs are offered as one example ('where customers face high costs'), not the sole condition, and the paragraph never calls early entry a handicap to avoid."),
    ("Business writers deliberately exaggerate the value of being first to market, picking pioneer stories that suit the lessons they already wish to teach.",
     "The paragraph describes a sampling bias toward survivors, not deliberate exaggeration; it attributes no motive to business writers.")],
   "The paragraph criticises the belief in first-mover advantage on two grounds: the stories behind it come from survivors, and followers have real benefits. It then concedes that early entry can matter 'where customers face high costs of switching' and concludes that 'the advantage is conditional'. The correct answer holds the critique and the concession together.",
   "Picking the follower option, which turns 'Followers also enjoy real benefits' into a general claim of superiority.",
   "V02D-first-mover-advantage",
   "The tempting wrong option reverses the myth instead of qualifying it; the correct answer must keep the concession.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-D-006", "Economics and business",
   "Counterfeits hurt luxury brands less by lost sales than by eroding the status signal of the genuine article.",
   "Difficult",
   "It might seem that counterfeits can only harm the luxury brands they copy, since each fake sold appears to be a sale lost. But most people who buy an obvious imitation for a small fraction of the price were never going to pay for the original, so the lost sale is often imaginary. The more serious threat is subtler. A luxury good is bought partly for what it signals, and the signal depends on the belief that the item is scarce and costly to obtain. When copies become common enough that observers can no longer tell who paid full price, the original loses part of what its buyers were paying for, even though none of them has been deceived. The harm, in other words, falls not on any transaction between the brand and the buyer of a fake but on the meaning of the logo for everyone else.",
   "Counterfeits harm luxury brands less through lost sales, often illusory, than by blurring which items are genuine and so weakening the status the logo signals.",
   [("Counterfeit luxury goods do little real harm to the brands they copy, because the people who buy fakes could never have paid for the genuine articles anyway.",
     "This keeps the first half of the argument and drops the 'more serious threat' to the meaning of the logo, so it contradicts the paragraph's conclusion that harm does occur."),
    ("The chief damage done by counterfeits falls on buyers of genuine luxury goods, who are misled into paying full price for items that are no longer scarce.",
     "The paragraph says genuine buyers lose part of what they paid for 'even though none of them has been deceived', so 'misled' contradicts it."),
    ("Luxury brands begin to lose sales to counterfeits once copies become common enough that observers can no longer tell genuine items from imitations.",
     "The paragraph locates the harm 'not on any transaction' but on the logo's meaning; recasting it as lost sales reverses the distinction it draws.")],
   "The paragraph first dismisses the obvious harm (lost sales are 'often imaginary'), then identifies 'The more serious threat': a luxury good's signal depends on perceived scarcity, and common copies blur who paid full price. Its last sentence locates the harm in 'the meaning of the logo for everyone else'. The correct answer states both moves.",
   "Choosing the lost-sales option because it mentions the right condition (copies becoming indistinguishable) while getting the kind of harm wrong.",
   "V02D-counterfeit-luxury-signal",
   "Every wrong option reuses the paragraph's terms; separating them needs the distinction between harm to sales, harm through deception and harm to a signal.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

ps("V02-D-007", "Economics and business",
   "Resentment of surge pricing reflects fairness norms about emergencies, not economic confusion, and ignoring it costs goodwill.",
   "Medium",
   "Economists tend to defend surge pricing on grounds of efficiency: when demand for rides or rooms spikes, a higher price rations the scarce supply to those who value it most and draws more suppliers in. The reasoning holds within its own terms, but it explains less of the public reaction than its defenders suppose. People who resent a tripled fare during a storm are not usually confused about supply and demand; they are objecting to what the price seems to say, that a seller is profiting from their predicament. Norms of fairness in many communities treat raising prices in an emergency differently from raising them on a busy Saturday, even when the economics are identical. A pricing scheme that ignores this distinction may be efficient on paper and still erode the goodwill on which the seller depends.",
   "Surge pricing may ration supply efficiently, but anger at it stems from fairness norms about profiting from emergencies, which sellers ignore at the cost of goodwill.",
   [("Public opposition to surge pricing arises mainly because customers misunderstand how higher prices ration scarce supply and attract extra sellers when demand spikes.",
     "The paragraph says resentful customers 'are not usually confused about supply and demand', so this states the opposite of its explanation."),
    ("Surge pricing is both unfair and inefficient in emergencies, so sellers should hold prices fixed whenever customers are caught in storms or similar predicaments.",
     "The paragraph grants that the efficiency reasoning 'holds within its own terms' and offers no rule about fixing prices, so both halves misstate it."),
    ("Customers accept higher prices on busy weekends but reject them in storms because the increases charged during emergencies tend to be far larger than weekend ones.",
     "The paragraph says the norms differ 'even when the economics are identical', so explaining the difference by the size of the increase contradicts it.")],
   "The paragraph accepts the efficiency case, then argues that it misses the source of public anger: people object to 'what the price seems to say', and fairness norms treat emergencies differently 'even when the economics are identical'. It ends by warning that such a scheme may 'erode the goodwill on which the seller depends'. The correct answer includes the concession, the explanation and the consequence.",
   "Choosing the 'unfair and inefficient' option by forgetting that the efficiency argument is conceded.",
   "V02D-surge-pricing-fairness",
   "Requires separating the conceded efficiency point from the fairness explanation; one wrong option contradicts each.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Science and environment
ps("V02-D-008", "Science and environment",
   "Suppressing every fire in fire-adapted forests lets fuel accumulate and produces larger fires.",
   "Easy",
   "In many forests that evolved with frequent low-intensity fire, decades of putting out every blaze have made fires worse rather than rarer. Small fires once cleared away dead wood, needles and young trees at regular intervals, keeping the fuel on the forest floor thin. When those fires were suppressed, the fuel kept accumulating, and dense undergrowth formed a ladder by which flames could climb into the canopy. A fire that would once have crept harmlessly along the ground now meets years of stored fuel and becomes the kind of blaze that kills mature trees and resists control. The policy succeeded at its immediate aim, stopping each fire, while creating the conditions for larger ones. The lesson applies chiefly to fire-adapted forests; in ecosystems where fire was naturally rare, suppression does not have the same effect.",
   "In forests adapted to frequent small fires, suppressing every blaze lets fuel pile up, so later fires grow larger and more destructive.",
   [("Putting out forest fires is always counterproductive, because fuel builds up in every kind of forest whenever small fires are prevented from burning.",
     "The last sentence restricts the point to fire-adapted forests and says that where fire was rare 'suppression does not have the same effect', so 'always' and 'every kind' contradict it."),
    ("Dense undergrowth lets flames climb from the forest floor into the canopy, where they kill mature trees and become very hard to bring under control.",
     "This describes one step in the mechanism and leaves out the cause, suppression of small fires, which is the paragraph's point."),
    ("Forest managers should stop fighting fires altogether and let every blaze burn, since small fires once kept forests thin and safe from larger ones.",
     "The paragraph explains a consequence of suppression but prescribes no policy, and letting every blaze burn goes well beyond anything it says.")],
   "The first sentence states the phenomenon, the middle sentences explain how fuel accumulates when small fires are suppressed, and the last two sentences draw the paradox and limit it to fire-adapted forests. The correct answer states the cause, the effect and the scope.",
   "Overlooking the final sentence's limit to fire-adapted forests.",
   "V02D-wildfire-suppression-paradox",
   "The point and its scope are stated plainly; wrong options are an overgeneralisation, a detail and a prescription.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-009", "Science and environment",
   "Repetition reveals precision but not accuracy; systematic error needs an independent check.",
   "Easy",
   "A measurement can be precise without being accurate. If a scale consistently reads two kilograms too heavy, repeated weighings of the same object will agree closely with one another, and that agreement may inspire confidence, yet every reading is wrong by the same amount. Precision describes how tightly repeated results cluster; accuracy describes how close they lie to the true value. The distinction matters because the usual way of checking a result, repeating it, reveals only the first. A systematic error survives any number of repetitions untouched, and can be exposed only by comparing the instrument with an independent standard or a different method. Consistency is therefore reassuring about random noise but silent about bias.",
   "Repeating a measurement shows whether results agree, not whether they are correct; systematic bias can be exposed only by an independent standard or method.",
   [("Precise instruments tend to be inaccurate, because the consistency of their readings conceals errors that less precise instruments would reveal through their scatter.",
     "The paragraph says precision and accuracy are independent ('can be precise without being accurate'), not that precise instruments tend to be inaccurate."),
    ("A scale that reads two kilograms too heavy gives readings that agree closely with one another, which is why such scales are trusted more than they deserve.",
     "This repeats the illustration without the general point it serves, that repetition cannot reveal systematic error."),
    ("Scientists should stop repeating measurements and rely only on comparisons with independent standards, since repetition cannot reveal any kind of error.",
     "The last sentence says consistency is 'reassuring about random noise', so repetition does reveal one kind of error, and the paragraph prescribes nothing.")],
   "The paragraph defines precision and accuracy, then explains that repetition 'reveals only the first', that a systematic error 'survives any number of repetitions' and can be exposed only by 'an independent standard or a different method'. The correct answer captures this distinction and its consequence.",
   "Choosing the option that stretches the paragraph into a rejection of repetition, missing that it still detects random noise.",
   "V02D-precision-versus-accuracy",
   "A single clearly stated distinction; wrong options misread, narrow or overstate it.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-010", "Science and environment",
   "Trees do cool streets, but only when mature and watered, so counting saplings planted overstates the relief.",
   "Medium",
   "Measurements in many cities have found that streets shaded by mature trees are noticeably cooler on summer afternoons than nearby streets without them, and planting trees has accordingly become a favoured response to urban heat. The finding is robust, but it is easy to draw more from it than it supports. Much of the cooling comes from shade and from water evaporating through leaves, and both depend on the tree being large, healthy and adequately watered; a newly planted sapling in a paved pit provides little of either for years, and in a drought a stressed tree may provide less still. Trees therefore help most where there is space, soil and water for them to mature, and planting programmes judged only by the number of saplings put in the ground may promise relief that arrives late or not at all.",
   "Mature, well-watered trees do cool city streets, but young or stressed trees give little cooling, so counting saplings planted overstates the relief that planting brings.",
   [("Planting trees is an ineffective answer to urban heat, since saplings in paved pits give almost no shade and stressed trees provide no cooling in a drought.",
     "The paragraph calls the cooling finding 'robust' and says trees 'help most' in suitable places, so calling planting ineffective contradicts it."),
    ("Streets shaded by mature trees are cooler on summer afternoons mainly because their leaves release water that evaporates and draws heat from the air around them.",
     "The paragraph credits both 'shade' and 'water evaporating through leaves', and in any case this mechanism is a detail, not the paragraph's point about overreading the finding."),
    ("Cities should plant trees only where soil and water are plentiful, and should abandon programmes that put saplings into narrow pits in paved streets.",
     "The paragraph warns against judging programmes by sapling counts but never tells cities to abandon any programme; the rule is an added prescription.")],
   "The paragraph states a finding (mature trees cool streets), affirms it, then qualifies what follows from it: cooling depends on size, health and water, so saplings give little for years. Its conclusion is that programmes 'judged only by the number of saplings' may over-promise. The correct answer keeps both the finding and the qualification.",
   "Treating the qualification as a refutation of the finding, or picking the mechanism detail.",
   "V02D-urban-tree-canopy",
   "A finding-and-qualification paragraph; the strongest wrong option overturns the finding instead of qualifying it.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-D-011", "Science and environment",
   "Because the fossil record is a sparse and biased sample, gaps in it cannot alone decide between gradual and abrupt change.",
   "Difficult",
   "It is sometimes argued that the scarcity of transitional fossils counts against gradual evolutionary change: if lineages changed slowly, the rocks should be full of intermediate forms. The argument assumes that the fossil record is a fair sample of past life, and it is not. Fossilisation requires rapid burial, suitable sediments and the absence of scavengers, conditions met for a tiny fraction of organisms and far more often for hard-shelled marine animals than for soft-bodied or upland ones. Erosion and the movement of the earth's crust have since destroyed much of what was preserved. Given a record this patchy, gaps are what one would expect under almost any account of change, so their presence cannot by itself decide between gradual and abrupt models. The question has to be settled by those parts of the record that are dense enough to test it.",
   "Since fossils form rarely and unevenly, gaps would appear under nearly any model of change, so only densely preserved sequences can show whether change was gradual.",
   [("The scarcity of transitional fossils shows that evolutionary change was abrupt rather than gradual, because slow change would have left many intermediate forms behind.",
     "This is the argument the paragraph reports and rejects, on the ground that it wrongly assumes the record 'is a fair sample of past life'."),
    ("The fossil record is too incomplete to tell us anything about how evolutionary change happened, so the dispute between gradual and abrupt models cannot be settled.",
     "The last sentence says the question can be settled by 'those parts of the record that are dense enough to test it', so declaring it unsettleable contradicts the paragraph."),
    ("Gaps in the fossil record arise chiefly because erosion and movements of the crust destroyed most fossils of soft-bodied and upland organisms after they had formed.",
     "The paragraph says such organisms rarely fossilised in the first place; erosion is a further cause, and in any case the option omits the conclusion about testing models.")],
   "The paragraph reports an argument from missing intermediates, rejects its assumption that the record is a fair sample, explains why it is sparse and biased, and concludes that gaps 'cannot by itself decide' the question, which must be settled by dense parts of the record. The correct answer captures the reason, the limited conclusion and the way forward.",
   "Choosing the 'too incomplete to tell us anything' option, which overstates the paragraph's scepticism and ignores its final sentence.",
   "V02D-fossil-record-gaps",
   "Two wrong options are close to the correct answer in different directions (the rejected argument, and excessive scepticism); the final sentence decides.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Technology
ps("V02-D-012", "Technology",
   "Software accumulates features because benefits are concentrated and costs diffuse, while removal creates visible losers.",
   "Easy",
   "Software products tend to accumulate features over time, and the process is hard to reverse. Each addition is usually requested by some group of users and can be justified on its own terms, while the costs it imposes are spread thinly: a slightly more crowded menu, a little more to test, one more interaction that can fail. Because no single feature seems to be the one that made the product cluttered, there is rarely a moment at which anyone decides to stop. Removing a feature, on the other hand, produces a visible loss for identifiable users, who complain. The result is a steady drift toward complexity that no one chose, driven by an imbalance between concentrated benefits and diffuse costs.",
   "Software grows cluttered because each feature has clear backers while its costs are small and scattered, and removal angers known users, so complexity builds unchosen.",
   [("Software companies add needless features on purpose in order to justify new versions, even when most users would much prefer a simpler and more reliable product.",
     "The paragraph describes a drift 'that no one chose', not a deliberate strategy, and it never mentions users preferring simplicity."),
    ("Every feature added to a software product makes it harder to test and more likely to fail, so developers ought to turn down most requests from users.",
     "The paragraph explains why features accumulate; it offers no advice to developers about refusing requests."),
    ("Users of software complain more about features being taken away than about menus becoming crowded, because people feel losses more keenly than gains.",
     "Complaints about removal are one part of the mechanism; the option drops the main point and adds a general claim about losses and gains.")],
   "The paragraph explains a phenomenon: additions have visible justifications while their costs are 'spread thinly', no single addition is decisive, and removal creates identifiable losers. Its last sentence names the cause, 'an imbalance between concentrated benefits and diffuse costs'. The correct answer restates this explanation.",
   "Choosing the deliberate-strategy option and missing 'that no one chose'.",
   "V02D-software-feature-creep",
   "The explanation is summarised in the final sentence; wrong options add a motive, a prescription or a narrow detail.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-013", "Technology",
   "Spreadsheets that inform big decisions should be reviewed like software, since their ease of use breeds unwarranted trust.",
   "Medium",
   "Spreadsheets are among the most widely used analytical tools in business and government, and also among the least scrutinised. A model built in one can be shared, extended and relied upon for years by people who never inspect its formulas, and a single misplaced cell reference can spread silently through every figure that depends on it. The danger is not that spreadsheets are unusually prone to error in the hands of careful users but that their ease of use invites a level of trust that their lack of checks does not justify. Code written by programmers is usually reviewed and tested; a spreadsheet that informs a large decision often has no comparable safeguard, simply because it does not look like software. Treating important spreadsheets as programs, subject to review, would not remove error, but it would bring their scrutiny closer to their influence.",
   "Important spreadsheets deserve software-style review, since their ease of use breeds trust their lack of checks does not warrant, though review would not end error.",
   [("Spreadsheets are more prone to error than programs written in code, so important decisions in business and government should not be based on them at all.",
     "The paragraph says the danger is 'not that spreadsheets are unusually prone to error', and it proposes reviewing them, not abandoning them."),
    ("A single misplaced cell reference can spread silently through every dependent figure, which is why spreadsheet models are usually discarded after a few years.",
     "The paragraph says models are relied upon 'for years'; the claim that they are discarded is invented, and the error-propagation detail is not the main point."),
    ("Reviewing spreadsheets in the way software is reviewed would eliminate the errors that now pass unnoticed into the decisions that rely on them.",
     "The last sentence says such review 'would not remove error'; it would only bring scrutiny closer to influence, so 'eliminate' contradicts it.")],
   "The paragraph identifies the gap between the influence of spreadsheets and the scrutiny they receive, locates the danger in misplaced trust rather than in unusual error rates, contrasts them with reviewed code, and recommends treating important spreadsheets as programs while conceding this 'would not remove error'. The correct answer keeps the recommendation, its reason and the concession.",
   "Picking the 'eliminate the errors' option by overlooking the concession in the final sentence.",
   "V02D-spreadsheet-errors",
   "The correct answer and one wrong option share the recommendation; only the correct answer respects the concession, and another wrong option reverses the 'not unusually prone' qualification.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-D-014", "Technology",
   "Batteries handle daily swings in wind and solar output well but not long lulls, so they are only part of the answer to intermittency.",
   "Medium",
   "Batteries are often presented as the answer to the intermittency of wind and solar power: store the surplus when the sun shines and release it when it does not. For smoothing variation over hours they are increasingly effective, absorbing the midday peak of solar output and covering the evening rise in demand. But the harder problem of intermittency is not the daily cycle. It is the windless, overcast week in winter, or the season in which output stays low for months, and covering gaps of that length with batteries of the kind now installed would require storage on a scale that is, at present, very costly. Batteries, then, solve one part of the problem well, and success with that part should not be mistaken for a solution to the whole, which will also need other means such as long-distance transmission, flexible demand or different forms of storage.",
   "Batteries smooth the hourly swings of wind and solar power well, but not, at present, lulls lasting weeks or seasons, so other measures will also be needed.",
   [("Batteries do little to solve the intermittency of wind and solar power, because the real difficulty lies in long winter lulls rather than in daily swings.",
     "The paragraph says batteries are 'increasingly effective' for hourly variation and 'solve one part of the problem well', so calling them of little help contradicts it."),
    ("Long-distance transmission and flexible demand deal with every form of intermittency in renewable electricity more effectively than batteries are able to.",
     "The paragraph lists these as additional means for the long gaps; it never ranks them above batteries, and certainly not for every form of intermittency."),
    ("Batteries already shift midday solar surplus into the evening, so continued investment in them will in time overcome the seasonal shortfalls of renewable power.",
     "The paragraph warns that success with the daily cycle 'should not be mistaken for a solution to the whole', which is the mistake this option makes.")],
   "The paragraph grants that batteries smooth hourly variation, then identifies the harder problem of week-long and seasonal gaps, which current batteries could cover only at very high cost, and concludes that the daily success 'should not be mistaken for a solution to the whole'. The correct answer keeps the concession, the limit and the need for other means.",
   "Choosing the option that dismisses batteries, which turns a limit on their role into a denial of it.",
   "V02D-grid-battery-intermittency",
   "Two wrong options err in opposite directions (too dismissive, too hopeful); the correct answer sits between them.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-D-015", "Technology",
   "Because spammers adapt to filters, filtering success decays and must be renewed, though filtering is not futile.",
   "Medium",
   "Spam filtering differs from most problems of classification in one important respect: the things being classified fight back. A filter that learns to recognise the features of unwanted messages changes the environment it was trained on, because senders whose messages are blocked alter them until they pass. Each improvement therefore tends to erode its own basis, and a filter that performed superbly when it was built may decline without any change to its code. This is why accuracy measured on yesterday's messages is a weak guide to tomorrow's, and why effective filtering is better understood as a continuing contest than as a solved problem. The point is not that filtering is futile, since raising the cost of evasion still reduces what gets through, but that its success has to be renewed rather than achieved once.",
   "Since senders adapt to what filters detect, filtering success decays and must be continually renewed; past accuracy predicts little, though filtering still cuts spam.",
   [("Spam filtering is ultimately futile, since every improvement in detection is soon cancelled out by senders who alter their messages in order to evade it.",
     "The last sentence says 'The point is not that filtering is futile', because raising the cost of evasion still reduces spam, so this contradicts the paragraph."),
    ("Spam filters lose accuracy over time mainly because their code grows outdated as email software and the habits of ordinary users gradually change.",
     "The paragraph says a filter may decline 'without any change to its code' and blames adapting senders, not outdated code or ordinary users."),
    ("Filters should be judged on future messages rather than past ones, because a filter trained on yesterday's messages will always misclassify those of tomorrow.",
     "The paragraph calls past accuracy 'a weak guide', not a guarantee of failure, so 'always misclassify' overstates it, and it proposes no rule for judging filters.")],
   "The paragraph explains why spam filtering is unusual: blocked senders adapt, so 'Each improvement therefore tends to erode its own basis'. It draws two consequences (past accuracy is 'a weak guide', filtering is 'a continuing contest') and adds a qualification, that filtering is not futile. The correct answer holds the mechanism, the consequence and the qualification.",
   "Choosing the futility option by missing the final qualification.",
   "V02D-spam-filter-arms-race",
   "The correct answer must keep a qualification that the most tempting wrong option denies; the others misplace the cause or overstate.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Arts and literature
ps("V02-D-016", "Arts and literature",
   "An unreliable narrator works only because a reliable author plants clues that let readers see past the narration.",
   "Medium",
   "A narrator who misleads the reader would seem to undermine the trust on which fiction depends, yet some of the most admired novels are told by narrators whose accounts cannot be taken at face value. The effect works because the author has not deceived the reader in the way the narrator has. Small inconsistencies, the reactions of other characters and gaps between what is said and what is shown allow an attentive reader to reconstruct what the narrator cannot or will not admit. The pleasure lies in that reconstruction, and in watching a mind reveal itself through the very effort of self-justification. An unreliable narrator, in other words, depends on a reliable author: if the clues were absent, the reader would simply be lied to, and the device would collapse into a trick.",
   "Unreliable narration rewards readers because the author plants clues that expose the narrator's distortions; without such clues the device would be mere deception.",
   [("Novels with unreliable narrators are admired more than other novels because readers enjoy being misled and then surprised by the truth near the end.",
     "The paragraph locates the pleasure in the reader's ongoing reconstruction, not in being misled and surprised, and it compares these novels with no others."),
    ("An unreliable narrator weakens the trust between author and reader, which is why only a few highly skilled novelists have attempted to use the device.",
     "The paragraph argues that the author 'has not deceived the reader', so trust between them survives; how many novelists use the device is never discussed."),
    ("Unreliable narrators reveal their characters through self-justification, which makes them more psychologically convincing than the narrators of novels who report events accurately and fully.",
     "Self-justification is one source of the pleasure the paragraph mentions, but the comparison with accurate narrators is invented and the central point about clues is missing.")],
   "The paragraph raises an apparent paradox (a misleading narrator in admired fiction), resolves it by distinguishing narrator from author, explains how clues let the reader reconstruct the truth, and concludes that 'An unreliable narrator ... depends on a reliable author'. The correct answer captures that resolution and its condition.",
   "Choosing the trust option, which repeats the apparent paradox the paragraph goes on to dissolve.",
   "V02D-unreliable-narrator",
   "The correct answer turns on the distinction between narrator and author; one wrong option restates the paradox as if it were the conclusion.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-D-017", "Arts and literature",
   "Item-by-item fidelity is a confused test of a film adaptation; the right test is comparable achievement in the film's own medium.",
   "Difficult",
   "Reviews of film adaptations often turn on fidelity: whether the film kept the plot, the characters and the ending of the book. The criterion is natural but confused. A novel can spend pages inside a character's thoughts, stretch a single moment or move freely across decades; a film must render most of this through image, sound and duration, and a scene transferred unchanged may carry quite different weight on screen. Literal faithfulness can therefore betray what made the book matter, while a film that alters events may preserve it. This does not make every departure justified, and some adaptations change a story for no better reason than convenience. But the question worth asking is whether the film achieves in its own medium something comparable to what the book achieved in its, not whether the two can be matched item by item.",
   "Since novels and films work differently, point-by-point fidelity is a poor test of an adaptation; what matters is whether the film achieves something comparable in its own medium.",
   [("Film adaptations should depart freely from their source books, since altering plot and characters is the only way to keep what made the original matter.",
     "The paragraph says only that a film that alters events 'may' preserve the book's value, and it adds that not 'every departure' is justified, so 'only way' and 'freely' overstate it."),
    ("Since films cannot convey the inner thoughts that novels describe at length, an adaptation of a novel is bound to lose what made the book worth reading.",
     "The paragraph says a film 'must render' inner life through image and sound, not that it cannot, and it argues that a film can achieve something comparable."),
    ("Reviewers judge adaptations by their fidelity because a film that keeps the plot, characters and ending of a book generally preserves its significance as well.",
     "The paragraph argues that 'Literal faithfulness can therefore betray what made the book matter', which is the reverse of this claim.")],
   "The paragraph rejects fidelity as 'natural but confused', explains why (the two media render thought, time and weight differently), concedes that some departures are unjustified, and proposes a better question: whether the film 'achieves in its own medium something comparable'. The correct answer states the rejection and the replacement test without overstating it.",
   "Choosing the 'depart freely' option, which keeps the criticism of fidelity but ignores the concession that not every change is justified.",
   "V02D-film-adaptation-fidelity",
   "The tempting wrong option is the correct answer's critique pushed one step too far; spotting it needs the concession in the second-last sentence.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

ps("V02-D-018", "Arts and literature",
   "Jazz improvisation is spontaneous recombination of a long-practised vocabulary, and that preparation makes spontaneity possible.",
   "Easy",
   "Improvisation in jazz is often imagined as pure spontaneity, music invented from nothing in the moment of performance. The reality is closer to the opposite. Improvisers spend years absorbing scales, harmonic patterns, phrases from recordings and the habits of other players, until a large vocabulary is available to them without conscious thought. What happens on stage is the fresh combination of this material in response to the other musicians and the shape of the tune, not creation from a blank page. Far from diminishing the achievement, this explains it: spontaneity of the kind audiences admire is possible only because so much has been prepared that the performer is free to listen and respond.",
   "Jazz improvisation is not invention from nothing but the live recombination of a long-practised musical vocabulary, and that preparation is what frees performers to respond spontaneously.",
   [("Jazz improvisation is far less spontaneous than audiences believe, which means that the achievement of improvising musicians has been greatly overestimated.",
     "The paragraph says the preparation is 'Far from diminishing the achievement', so concluding that it is overestimated contradicts it."),
    ("Jazz musicians learn phrases from recordings and from other players so that they can reproduce them exactly whenever they perform before an audience.",
     "The paragraph speaks of 'the fresh combination of this material', not exact reproduction, so this distorts how the learned phrases are used."),
    ("Improvising musicians respond to one another and to the shape of the tune, which is why the same jazz piece sounds different every time it is played.",
     "Responding to other players is one detail; the paragraph's point about preparation making spontaneity possible is missing, and the claim about repeated pieces is added.")],
   "The paragraph corrects a common belief (improvisation as invention from nothing), explains what improvisers actually prepare, and concludes that preparation is what makes admired spontaneity possible. The correct answer captures the correction and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the 'overestimated' option by reading the correction as a debunking.",
   "V02D-jazz-improvisation-preparation",
   "The point is stated directly; the main wrong option reverses the final sentence.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-019", "Arts and literature",
   "Both sides of the varnish-cleaning dispute appeal to the artist's intention; they differ over trust in present reconstructions.",
   "Medium",
   "Few decisions in the care of old paintings provoke as much argument as the removal of discoloured varnish. Those who favour cleaning point out that yellowed varnish distorts colour and flattens depth, so that a viewer sees the grime of centuries rather than the painting. Those who resist it reply that cleaning is irreversible, that it can strip away thin final glazes the artist applied, and that our idea of what the painting originally looked like is itself a guess. Both sides appeal to the artist's intention, which is part of what makes the dispute so stubborn: the cleaner trusts present knowledge to recover it, the sceptic distrusts that knowledge enough to leave the surface alone. What separates them is less a disagreement about values than about how much confidence our reconstructions deserve.",
   "Advocates and opponents of cleaning old paintings both aim to honour the artist's intention; they differ mainly over how far present knowledge can reconstruct the original.",
   [("Discoloured varnish should be left in place, because cleaning cannot be reversed and may strip away the thin final glazes that the artist applied.",
     "This adopts the sceptics' side, while the paragraph reports both positions without endorsing either and locates the dispute in confidence, not in a verdict."),
    ("The dispute over cleaning old paintings sets those who value the artist's original intention against those who value the marks that age leaves on a surface.",
     "The paragraph says 'Both sides appeal to the artist's intention'; neither side is described as valuing the marks of age, so the opposition is invented."),
    ("Because yellowed varnish distorts colour and flattens depth, cleaning lets viewers see old paintings as they first appeared rather than the grime of centuries.",
     "This is the cleaners' argument presented as fact; the paragraph notes that our idea of the original appearance 'is itself a guess'.")],
   "The paragraph sets out the case for and against cleaning, then observes that 'Both sides appeal to the artist's intention' and concludes that the disagreement is 'less ... about values than about how much confidence our reconstructions deserve'. The correct answer states this shared aim and the real point of difference.",
   "Choosing the intention-versus-age option, which sounds like a neat summary of a dispute but contradicts 'Both sides appeal to the artist's intention'.",
   "V02D-varnish-removal-paintings",
   "A comparison of two views; two wrong options each state one side, and a third misdescribes what divides them.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-D-020", "Arts and literature",
   "The literary canon is shaped by institutional access and sustained by readers' continued use.",
   "Difficult",
   "Defenders of the literary canon sometimes speak as if it were simply the record of quality, the works that survived because they were best. Critics reply that it is the record of power, the works that those with influence over schools, publishers and reviews chose to preserve. Each picture captures something and each is too simple. Survival does depend on institutions, and works by people excluded from those institutions often failed to be printed, taught or kept; to that extent the canon reflects who was in a position to choose. But institutions do not keep works alive by decree alone. A book that generations of readers find inert will drop out of the curriculum however prestigious its origins, and the works that last tend to be those that keep rewarding new readers with questions their makers never imagined. The canon is shaped by access and sustained by use.",
   "The canon reflects both institutional power, which decided whose works were preserved and taught, and readers' continuing engagement, which decides which of them stay alive.",
   [("The literary canon is above all a record of power, since works by people shut out of schools, publishers and reviews were never printed, taught or preserved.",
     "The paragraph calls this picture 'too simple' and says excluded works 'often' failed to survive, not that they never did; it adds that institutions cannot keep works alive 'by decree alone'."),
    ("The works that remain in the literary canon are those of the highest quality, because books that readers find inert sooner or later drop out of the curriculum.",
     "This is the defenders' picture, which the paragraph also calls 'too simple'; it ignores the role of access in deciding which works were ever kept and taught."),
    ("Prestigious institutions keep the canon alive by deciding which books are taught, but readers now increasingly reject those choices in favour of newer works.",
     "The paragraph says institutions do not keep works alive 'by decree alone', and it reports no trend of readers rejecting canonical choices for newer works.")],
   "The paragraph sets two pictures of the canon against each other, calls each 'too simple', grants the power view its due (exclusion limited what survived) and the quality view its due (inert books drop out), and concludes that 'The canon is shaped by access and sustained by use'. The correct answer restates that synthesis.",
   "Choosing one of the two pictures the paragraph explicitly calls 'too simple'.",
   "V02D-literary-canon-formation",
   "Two wrong options each capture half the synthesis with plausible support; only the correct answer keeps both halves and their division of labour.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Language and media
ps("V02-D-021", "Language and media",
   "Borrowing words rarely threatens a language; languages die when speakers abandon them.",
   "Easy",
   "Campaigns to protect a language from foreign words usually rest on the fear that borrowing will dilute it or eventually destroy it. The history of languages gives little support to this fear. Most major languages have absorbed large numbers of words from their neighbours, conquerors and trading partners, and they have remained recognisably themselves, because what makes a language distinct lies far more in its grammar and sound system than in the origin of its vocabulary. Borrowed words are typically reshaped to fit the borrowing language's pronunciation and grammatical endings until speakers no longer notice where they came from. Languages do die, but they die when their speakers abandon them for another language, not when they take in words from outside.",
   "Taking in foreign words seldom endangers a language, whose identity lies mainly in grammar and sound; languages die when their speakers abandon them, not through borrowing.",
   [("Campaigns to keep foreign words out of a language do real harm, because a language that refuses to borrow will lose its speakers to its neighbours and die.",
     "The paragraph says languages die when speakers abandon them, but it never claims that refusing to borrow causes this; the causal link is invented."),
    ("Most major languages consist largely of borrowed words, which shows that vocabulary has no connection at all with what makes a language distinct.",
     "The paragraph says distinctness lies 'far more' in grammar and sound, not that vocabulary has 'no connection' to it, and it says languages absorbed many words, not that they consist largely of them."),
    ("Borrowed words are usually reshaped to fit the pronunciation and grammar of the language that takes them in, until speakers forget where the words came from.",
     "This repeats one supporting detail and leaves out the paragraph's point, that borrowing does not threaten a language's survival.")],
   "The paragraph states a fear (borrowing destroys languages), rejects it with historical evidence and an explanation (identity lies in grammar and sound; loans are adapted), and ends by naming what does kill languages. The correct answer captures the rejection, its reason and the contrast in the last sentence.",
   "Choosing the reshaping option because it is a true statement from the paragraph.",
   "V02D-loanwords-linguistic-purity",
   "The point is explicit in the second and last sentences; wrong options add a cause, overstate, or narrow.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-022", "Language and media",
   "Emoji supply the tone that casual writing lacks, so judging them by formal prose misunderstands them.",
   "Easy",
   "Complaints that emoji are degrading written language tend to miss what they are for. Spoken conversation carries much of its meaning in tone of voice, facial expression and gesture, which tell a listener whether a remark is a joke, a rebuke or a kindness. Casual written messages, exchanged quickly between people who know each other, lost most of these signals, and misunderstandings followed: a brief reply could read as cold, an ironic one as sincere. Emoji and similar marks restore part of that missing layer, which is why they cluster in informal messages and are rare in contracts or reports. They are less a replacement for words than a substitute for the face, and judging them by the standards of formal prose mistakes their function.",
   "Emoji chiefly restore the tone and expression that quick informal messages lose, so condemning them by the standards of formal prose misunderstands their purpose.",
   [("Emoji have improved written language by replacing many words with symbols that express meaning more quickly and more precisely than formal prose can.",
     "The paragraph says emoji are 'less a replacement for words than a substitute for the face', so describing them as replacing words reverses its point."),
    ("Because casual written messages are often misread, people ought to add emoji whenever a remark they write might be taken in the wrong way.",
     "The paragraph explains why emoji exist and where they appear; it gives no advice about when people should use them."),
    ("Emoji are acceptable in informal messages between friends, but they degrade written language now that they are spreading into contracts and formal reports.",
     "The paragraph says emoji 'are rare in contracts or reports' and rejects the complaint that they degrade language, so both halves contradict it.")],
   "The paragraph rebuts a complaint by explaining the function of emoji: casual writing lost the cues of tone, face and gesture, and emoji 'restore part of that missing layer'. It concludes that 'judging them by the standards of formal prose mistakes their function'. The correct answer states both the function and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the 'improved written language' option by confusing a defence of emoji with a claim that they replace words.",
   "V02D-emoji-tone",
   "The function and conclusion are stated directly; each wrong option contradicts a specific sentence or adds advice.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-023", "Language and media",
   "Sign languages are full natural languages, and treating them as lesser codes has harmed deaf education.",
   "Easy",
   "Sign languages are often assumed to be a gestured version of the spoken language around them, or a simple system of mime that anyone could follow. Both assumptions are mistaken. Sign languages used by deaf communities have their own grammars, with rules for word order, agreement and the use of space that differ from those of the local spoken language, and they cannot be understood by untrained hearing observers any more than an unfamiliar spoken language can. They also vary from country to country and change over time, as all natural languages do. Treating them as lesser codes has had practical costs, since it has encouraged schooling that suppressed signing in favour of lip-reading and speech.",
   "Sign languages are complete natural languages with their own grammars, not gestured speech or mime, and regarding them as lesser has harmed the schooling of deaf people.",
   [("Sign languages form a universal system of gestures that deaf people from every country can use to communicate with one another without any training.",
     "The paragraph says sign languages 'vary from country to country' and cannot be followed by the untrained, so this states the assumption it rejects."),
    ("Deaf children learn better through sign language than through lip-reading and speech, so schools should teach them only in sign language from the start.",
     "The paragraph criticises schooling that suppressed signing but makes no comparison of learning outcomes and recommends no exclusive policy."),
    ("Sign languages follow the word order and grammar of the spoken language of their country, but they use space and movement where speech uses sound.",
     "The paragraph says their grammars 'differ from those of the local spoken language', which is the opposite of following its word order and grammar.")],
   "The paragraph names two mistaken assumptions, refutes them (independent grammars, unintelligible without training, variation and change) and ends with a practical consequence of the error for schooling. The correct answer keeps the refutation and the consequence.",
   "Choosing the schooling option because it echoes the last sentence while adding a comparison and a policy.",
   "V02D-sign-languages-natural",
   "The point is stated in the second sentence and supported plainly; wrong options restate the rejected views or add a prescription.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

ps("V02-D-024", "Language and media",
   "Radio drama's lack of images is both its strength and its limitation.",
   "Medium",
   "Radio drama is sometimes regarded as a poor relation of film and television, a form that lasted only until pictures could be added. Yet the absence of images is not simply a lack. Because the listener must supply faces, rooms and landscapes, each production is completed in a different imagination, and the scenes that would be most expensive or least convincing on screen, a battle at sea or a city on another planet, cost a radio producer little more than a few sounds and a sentence. What radio cannot do is control exactly what the audience sees, and so it suits stories that depend on voice, suggestion and inner life better than those that depend on precise visual spectacle. Its limitations and its strengths are, in large part, the same feature viewed from two sides.",
   "Lacking pictures both frees radio drama, letting listeners imagine any setting cheaply, and limits it, suiting stories of voice and suggestion over precise spectacle.",
   [("Radio drama is superior to film and television, because listeners can picture any scene, however vast or costly, without the limits that images impose.",
     "The paragraph presents the absence of images as both a strength and a limit; it never ranks radio above film and television."),
    ("Radio drama lasted only until pictures could be added to broadcasts, after which film and television replaced it for most of its former audience.",
     "This is the dismissive view the paragraph reports and then disputes with 'Yet the absence of images is not simply a lack'."),
    ("Radio producers favour scenes such as sea battles and alien cities because these can be created with a few sounds and at very little expense.",
     "The paragraph says such scenes cost radio little, not that producers favour them, and the option drops the limitation entirely.")],
   "The paragraph disputes the view of radio as a poor relation, shows how the absence of images is a strength (imagination, low cost of spectacle) and a limitation (no control over what is seen), and concludes that these are 'the same feature viewed from two sides'. The correct answer keeps both sides and the resulting fit with certain stories.",
   "Choosing the 'superior' option, which keeps the strength and forgets the limitation.",
   "V02D-radio-drama-imagination",
   "A comparison with a two-sided conclusion; the strongest wrong option keeps only one side.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

ps("V02-D-025", "Economics and business",
   "The conglomerate discount alone cannot show that diversification destroys value, because selection may explain part of it.",
   "Difficult",
   "Many analyses of stock markets have reported that diversified conglomerates tend to be valued below what their separate divisions would be worth as independent firms, and the finding has often been read as proof that diversification destroys value. The reading moves too quickly. Firms do not diversify at random: companies that expand into unrelated businesses may already have been struggling in their core activity, and the divisions conglomerates acquire may have been weaker than the independent firms used as benchmarks. If so, part of the discount reflects who chooses to diversify and what they buy, not damage done by diversification itself. None of this shows that conglomerates are efficient; managers of sprawling firms may well misallocate capital among divisions. It shows only that a lower valuation, on its own, cannot tell the two explanations apart.",
   "A conglomerate's lower valuation need not mean diversifying destroyed value, since weaker firms and divisions may be the ones that diversify; the discount alone cannot separate selection from harm.",
   [("Diversified conglomerates are worth less than their separate divisions because the managers of sprawling firms misallocate capital among businesses they do not understand.",
     "The paragraph offers misallocation only as something that 'may well' happen, and its point is that the discount 'cannot tell the two explanations apart', so asserting this cause contradicts it."),
    ("The discount reported for conglomerates is an illusion produced by comparing weak acquired divisions with strong independent firms, so diversification does no harm.",
     "The paragraph says 'part of the discount' may reflect selection and that 'None of this shows that conglomerates are efficient', so 'illusion' and 'no harm' overstate it."),
    ("Firms that are struggling in their core activity ought to avoid expanding into unrelated businesses, because doing so pushes their market valuation lower still.",
     "The paragraph gives no advice to firms and asserts no causal effect of diversifying on valuation; that is exactly what it says the evidence cannot establish.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (the conglomerate discount), rejects the inference that diversification destroys value, offers a selection explanation, concedes that conglomerates may still misallocate capital, and concludes that the discount alone 'cannot tell the two explanations apart'. The correct answer keeps the finding, the alternative and the limited conclusion.",
   "Choosing the 'illusion' option, which turns the selection argument into a full acquittal and ignores the concession.",
   "V02D-conglomerate-diversification-discount",
   "Two wrong options each assert one of the explanations the paragraph says cannot be separated; the correct answer must stay agnostic between them.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)
